sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, one Swamp run, agent-observer-swamp-acts, root-of-trust-gateway-only, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#add-a-node.
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: node:add
  %% kind: node.add
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as New Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant HUB as WireGuard hub<br/>vpn Node
  CLI->>GW: node:add NAME HOST<br/>--host-key-fingerprint=SHA256
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:add NAME HOST --host-key-fingerprint=SHA256`
  %%> - syntax: `<name> [host] [--host-key-fingerprint=SHA256] [--ssh-port=PORT] [--user=USER] [--orbit-user=USER] [--platform=linux|macos] [--architecture=ARCH] [--tld=TLD] [--role=ROLE]… [--cluster=ID] [--wireguard-ip=IP] [--lan-ip=IP] [--wireguard-endpoint=HOST:PORT] [--dns-server=IP] [--setting=PATH:VALUE] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node name, its public SSH host, and the approved SSH host key fingerprint; a new Node, or an existing Node with no pinned host key, needs all three
  %%> - every other option is recorded in the intent; only `--role` and `--platform=macos` add steps
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds the machine to the fleet as a Node
  %%> - for a Node that already exists, the same command converges it again with its new TLD, roles or settings
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, record the Node
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the name is free, or names a Node that owns no Instances (`node.has_instances`)
  %%> - a new Node, or an existing Node with no pinned host key, has a host and a host key fingerprint (`node.ssh_host_fingerprint_required`); for an existing Node the fingerprint equals the recorded one (`node.ssh_host_key_mismatch`)
  %%> - Linux accepts only `x86_64`; `app-dev` needs a TLD, unless its Cluster has one
  %%> - macOS names one existing account in `--user` and `--orbit-user`, an existing WireGuard address, and no role
  %%> - no other lifecycle change runs for the name; the Gateway holds one lock per Node name
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a new Node is recorded as `provisioning`; an existing Node keeps its record until the result
  %%> - the WireGuard address and the hub peer entry are chosen
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent; nothing has happened on the machine yet
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-add
  %%> Secrets:
  %%> - the bootstrap SSH identity and the new Node agent secret travel only through the vault, under one key name per Node; the run inputs hold key names, never values
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the host, port, bootstrap user, managed user and approved fingerprint
  %%> - packages, pins, Node agent configuration and the frozen role resources
  %%> - the hub's address and SSH host keys, and the new peer entry
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task ID, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Check the host key<br/>SSH argv · native assert
  %%> Why first:
  %%> - first contact ignores the worker's known hosts, so nothing on the machine changes until the host key matches the approved fingerprint
  SW->>N: Authorize the control key<br/>SSH argv
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - appends the Swamp control public key to the bootstrap account when it is absent
  %%> - pins the machine's host key for the run
  SW->>N: Set up the machine<br/>Ansible phase: baseline
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - base packages, the managed account, a sudo rule that is validated before it replaces the old one, so no staged file stays, and the Orbit SSH keys
  %%> - the public recovery SSH rule, then the firewall
  %%> Linux machines:
  %%> - with `--platform=macos` the Linux steps are skipped and `opt --platform=macos` runs instead
  SW->>N: Set up the tunnel<br/>Ansible phase: tunnel
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates the WireGuard key, writes the tunnel files and starts the tunnel
  %%> - sets the tunnel address and trusts the WireGuard members
  %%> Secrets:
  %%> - the private key stays in a key file on the Node; only the public key leaves it
  SW->>SW: Require unique tunnel key<br/>native assert
  %%> Why:
  %%> - two Nodes with the same public key cannot both be peers
  opt Linux peer joins the VPN hub
    SW->>HUB: Add the peer<br/>Ansible phase: hub
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - writes the peer entry and applies the hub peers
    %%> - the hub peer stays after a failure; it is harmless and a rerun reuses it
  end
  SW->>N: Install the Node agent<br/>Ansible phase: agent
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - installs the pinned `orbit-agent` release, its configuration and the observer secret, and starts it
  SW->>N: Verify managed access<br/>SSH argv
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - logs in as the managed user and runs a passwordless sudo command
  opt eligible CLI plan
    SW->>N: Publish the Orbit CLI<br/>native child workflow
    %%> Best effort:
    %%> - a failure here does not fail the add
  end
  opt --role
    SW->>N: Converge each role<br/>native child workflow
    %%> Root boundary:
    %%> - roles are frozen child-workflow inputs in this run, with no child tasks
    %%> The first role:
    %%> - closes public recovery SSH, so the Node is reachable over WireGuard only afterwards
    %%> - the `vpn` role also enables forwarding on the hub
  end
  opt DNS needs publication
    SW->>N: Publish private DNS<br/>native child workflow
    %%> When:
    %%> - a new VPN hub needs its DNS listener first, or private DNS records need publication
  end
  opt --platform=macos
    SW->>N: Verify managed SSH<br/>SSH argv
    SW->>N: Inspect the Mac tunnel<br/>SSH argv
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - macOS runs the host key check, these two steps and a native check; it has no sudo check, no Ansible check phase and no handshake read
  end
  SW->>N: Check the Node<br/>Ansible phase: check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the agent and its binary run, the observer secret is in place, and the tunnel handshakes with the hub; the hub itself is exempt
  %%> - it is the one end check the Gateway reads
  alt every step passes
    N-->>SW: arch · host key · WG key
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the architecture, the host key and the WireGuard public key
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the Node active
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Node becomes active, with its architecture, SSH host key pins and WireGuard key
    %%> - each converged role becomes active; storage settings are stored
    %%> - the task succeeds
    %%> Next:
    %%> - Metrics exporters start only with `metrics:exporter:enable`
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or mismatch
    %%> When:
    %%> - the host key or architecture differs, the bootstrap or a role fails, the hub refuses its configuration, or the check fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Mark the Node failed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - a new Node stays, marked failed at that step, so you can see it
    %%> - an existing Node keeps its earlier record and stays active
    %%> - a role created by this request that did not finish stays marked failed, and the rerun converges it
    %%> - the task fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same `node:add` again after fixing the cause; every step skips work that is already done
    %%> Removal:
    %%> - `node:remove` removes a failed Node
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
  end
